<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE node PUBLIC
 "-//freedesktop//DTD D-BUS Object Introspection 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/dbus/1.0/introspect.dtd">

<!--
  Interface definition for idlepolicyd.

    bus name     io.github.ericcanas.Idlectl1
    object path  /io/github/ericcanas/Idlectl1
    interface    io.github.ericcanas.Idlectl1.Manager

  Installed at /usr/share/dbus-1/interfaces/ so that other projects can generate
  bindings without depending on this one.

  Time is in MICROSECONDS on CLOCK_BOOTTIME, matching logind's own convention for
  IdleSinceHint and friends, and UINT64_MAX means "never". Two deliberate choices:

    * CLOCK_BOOTTIME, not CLOCK_MONOTONIC, because MONOTONIC stops during suspend and
      every deadline computed before a sleep would come back wrong by exactly the
      length of the sleep. Not CLOCK_REALTIME either: NTP can step it, and a backwards
      step makes a deadline unreachable.
    * UINT64_MAX rather than 0 for "never", because 0 is a real instant (boot) and
      because a client that forgets to special-case it gets a deadline absurdly far in
      the future (a machine that stays awake, which is the cheap error) instead of
      one already in the past.
-->
<node>
  <interface name="io.github.ericcanas.Idlectl1.Manager">

    <!-- ============================ properties ============================ -->

    <!-- Daemon version string, the same one the binary reports on the command line. -->
    <property name="Version" type="s" access="read"/>

    <!--
      Configuration layers actually applied, in order. The answer to "which file set
      this?", which is otherwise guesswork once drop-ins are involved.
    -->
    <property name="ConfigLayers" type="as" access="read"/>

    <!--
      Every fact and its current state, as (name, state) pairs. State is one of
      "true", "false", "indeterminate", "unavailable".

      The last two are not interchangeable and clients must not collapse them:
      "unavailable" means this machine has no such capability (or that the fact was
      switched off by name in the configuration), and behaves as false;
      "indeterminate" means the detector exists and could not answer, which vetoes
      suspend, hibernate and poweroff. The veto does not depend on any block naming
      that fact: it is about the machine no longer being able to see something it
      normally sees. Any doubt is a veto, except for screen_off, where it is not,
      because one unreadable detector must not be able to keep an OLED panel lit all
      night.

      A capability that is absent always reads "unavailable" and never
      "indeterminate". Absence is knowledge; only a detector that exists and failed
      produces doubt.
    -->
    <property name="Facts" type="a(ss)" access="read"/>

    <!--
      Per-action resolution, as (action, deadline_usec, due) triples. `action` is one
      of "screen_off", "suspend", "hibernate", "poweroff".

      Each deadline is resolved independently: the LATEST deadline among the true
      [while] blocks that set a key for that action (plus any implicit floor, such as
      an indeterminate fact or a rejected configuration file), then pulled earlier by
      the EARLIEST deadline among the true [when] blocks that set one. More than one
      may be due at the same time; the daemon then performs screen_off if it is due
      plus the SHALLOWEST due sleep action, never a deeper one. A client must not
      read several due sleep actions as "all of these will happen".
    -->
    <property name="Deadlines" type="a(stb)" access="read"/>

    <!--
      The earliest deadline not yet reached: when the daemon will next act if nothing
      changes. UINT64_MAX when nothing is scheduled.
    -->
    <property name="NextDeadlineUSec" type="t" access="read"/>

    <!-- Whether the daemon is evaluating but deliberately not acting. -->
    <property name="DryRun" type="b" access="read"/>

    <!-- ============================= methods ============================== -->

    <!--
      Human-readable explanation of one action, or of all four when `action` is empty.
      Names the composed deadline, the clock each block was measured on, and every
      block holding the action back.

      It also names the reasons that are not blocks: every enabled fact currently
      reading indeterminate, and every configuration file that had to be rejected.
      A machine wedged awake by one of those has nothing in its configuration to point
      at, so leaving them out would make the one case that most needs explaining the
      one case with no explanation.

      Not polkit-checked. A diagnostic that needs authentication is a diagnostic
      nobody runs.
    -->
    <method name="Explain">
      <arg name="action" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="text" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!--
      Which detectors work here and which capabilities are absent. The supported way
      to tell "unavailable" from "indeterminate" without reading the journal.
    -->
    <method name="Doctor">
      <arg name="text" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <!--
        Whether everything is in order. The client's exit status is THIS, never a
        re-reading of the text: [OBS-4] makes doctor non-zero if any configuration
        fault, any indeterminate fact, or any standing hazard is present — three
        conditions, not one — and a client that grepped the prose to decide would
        disagree with the daemon the first time a word changed.
      -->
      <arg name="healthy" type="b" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!--
      The same information Explain() prints, as a JSON document.

      This is what an external caller reads: a relay that woke this machine and needs
      to know whether it may put it back to sleep, a monitoring check, a desktop
      applet. It carries every fact with its state and its evidence, every action with
      its floor, ceiling, resolved deadline and what is holding it, the configuration
      layers in force, any faults, and the leases held.

      The top-level `schema` integer is the contract. Fields may be ADDED without
      changing it; a field that changes meaning or disappears bumps it. Read `schema`
      before anything else and refuse a number you do not know — guessing at a
      document you cannot parse is how a relay ends up suspending a machine because a
      field it wanted was missing and defaulted to zero.

      Not polkit-checked, for the same reason as Explain().
    -->
    <method name="Report">
      <arg name="json" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!--
      Treat exactly two blocks as satisfied (the one whose condition is `always` and
      the one whose condition is `after_resume`), as if their timeouts had elapsed,
      including where their configured value is `never`. The base schedule and the
      post-resume settle window, and nothing else. The set is fixed by condition name
      and no configuration key can enlarge it.

      IT DOES NOT COLLAPSE CONDITION BLOCKS. A running game, an active download, a
      held lease and an open remote session are all still evaluated normally, each on
      its own clock, and any of them can still refuse. Neither does it satisfy the
      vetoes that are not blocks: an indeterminate fact and a rejected configuration
      file both still hold. This is what a remote relay calls when it has finished
      with a machine; it must never be able to suspend one that is in the middle of
      something.

      Returns whether the action became due. `false` is a normal answer, not an error:
      it means something else is still holding the machine awake, and Explain() will
      say what.

      polkit: io.github.ericcanas.Idlectl1.rest
    -->
    <method name="Rest">
      <!--
        "screen_off", "suspend", "hibernate" or "poweroff". An EMPTY string means
        suspend, always: there is no configurable default rest action, and with
        poweroff requiring the word to be typed, no request can close somebody's
        session unless its sender said so.
      -->
      <arg name="action" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="accepted" type="b" direction="out"/>
      <annotation name="io.github.ericcanas.Idlectl1.PolkitAction"
                  value="io.github.ericcanas.Idlectl1.rest"/>
    </method>

    <!--
      Ask, and be remembered if the answer is "not yet" — REQ-6.

      Returns TRUE only if the action happened immediately. FALSE means the request is
      being HELD, not that it was refused: the machine carries it out the moment the
      last veto clears, and forgets it at the TTL.

      A held request is not a weaker one. It is re-evaluated on the ordinary schedule
      with exactly the floors the original had, so a game that starts after it was made
      refuses it just as surely as one already running would have. What is remembered
      is the asking, never the answer.

      It is dropped early if somebody uses the machine (REQ-7). Waking from sleep is
      not somebody using the machine (REQ-8), so a box woken by a relay to run a job
      does not cancel the request that will let it sleep again afterwards.

      This is what a relay wants: "I am done with this machine, rest when you can."
      Without it the caller has to poll, which means owning a timer, a retry budget and
      its own copy of "is it still worth asking" — three things this daemon has already.

      polkit: io.github.ericcanas.Idlectl1.rest — the same action as Rest(). Holding a
      request reaches nothing Rest() does not; it only keeps trying.
    -->
    <method name="RestPending">
      <arg name="action" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <!-- Microseconds. Zero is an error: use Rest() to ask exactly once. -->
      <arg name="ttl_usec" type="t" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="performed_now" type="b" direction="out"/>
      <annotation name="io.github.ericcanas.Idlectl1.PolkitAction"
                  value="io.github.ericcanas.Idlectl1.rest"/>
    </method>

    <!--
      Forget a held request. Idempotent; FALSE means there was nothing to forget.
    -->
    <method name="CancelPending">
      <arg name="had_one" type="b" direction="out"/>
      <annotation name="io.github.ericcanas.Idlectl1.PolkitAction"
                  value="io.github.ericcanas.Idlectl1.rest"/>
    </method>

    <!--
      Install a ceiling due immediately for this one action and this one request, and
      perform it.

      Ceilings are absolute, so this defeats every floor there is, mechanically and
      without special cases: every `never`, the human_active floor, the veto raised by
      a fact reading indeterminate, and the veto raised by a configuration file that
      had to be rejected. The last two are what it is usually reached for: a machine
      wedged awake by something broken rather than by something happening, which
      Rest() by construction cannot help.

      It overrides POLICY, never MECHANISM. One power transition at a time still
      holds, a refusal from logind is still logged rather than retried blindly, and
      the facts are still re-read and the policy recomputed immediately before acting,
      carrying this same ephemeral ceiling, so a forced action cannot cancel itself.

      Deliberately a separate method rather than a flag on Rest(): a caller has to
      have chosen this one by name, the polkit action is separate, and `why` is
      mandatory and recorded in the journal at warning level together with every floor
      that was defeated and each floor's reason. This is the only way to suspend a
      machine somebody is actively typing on.

      polkit: io.github.ericcanas.Idlectl1.rest-forced
    -->
    <method name="RestForced">
      <arg name="action" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="why" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <annotation name="io.github.ericcanas.Idlectl1.PolkitAction"
                  value="io.github.ericcanas.Idlectl1.rest-forced"/>
    </method>

    <!--
      Take a lease: the machine will not sleep while it is held.

      Returns a file descriptor, the way logind's Inhibit() does, and for the same
      reason: the lease is released when the descriptor is closed, so a job that
      crashes or is killed cannot pin a machine awake forever. `ttl_usec` is a second
      bound on top of that, for the case where the holder survives but forgets.

      polkit: io.github.ericcanas.Idlectl1.lease
    -->
    <method name="AcquireLease">
      <arg name="who" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="why" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="ttl_usec" type="t" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="handle" type="h" direction="out"/>
      <annotation name="io.github.ericcanas.Idlectl1.PolkitAction"
                  value="io.github.ericcanas.Idlectl1.lease"/>
    </method>

    <!--
      Release a lease by name, before its handle is closed and before its TTL expires.

      Returns whether one was held. Releasing only ever permits the machine to sleep,
      which its schedule was going to do anyway, so this is authorised by the same
      lease action rather than by one of its own.

      polkit: io.github.ericcanas.Idlectl1.lease
    -->
    <method name="ReleaseLease">
      <arg name="who" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="released" type="b" direction="out"/>
      <annotation name="io.github.ericcanas.Idlectl1.PolkitAction"
                  value="io.github.ericcanas.Idlectl1.lease"/>
    </method>

    <!--
      Leases currently held, as (who, why, acquired_usec, expires_usec, uid).
      Not polkit-checked: seeing what is holding a machine awake is diagnosis.
    -->
    <method name="ListLeases">
      <arg name="leases" type="a(sttu)" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!--
      Re-read the configuration. Returns the layers that were applied.

      A file that cannot be parsed, or that carries a value the format does not
      accept, is dropped AS A WHOLE: per file, never per key, because a half-applied
      file is a policy nobody wrote. The layers that still parse take effect.
      While any such fault stands, suspend, hibernate and poweroff are held at
      `never`, screen_off is deliberately not, and Doctor() exits non-zero naming the
      file, the key and the text it could not read.

      The daemon therefore never ends up with no policy at all, including on a first
      start after a bad edit, when there is no previous policy to fall back to. A
      machine with no owner of its power state is the condition this project exists to
      remove.

      polkit: io.github.ericcanas.Idlectl1.reload
    -->
    <method name="Reload">
      <arg name="layers" type="as" direction="out"/>
      <annotation name="io.github.ericcanas.Idlectl1.PolkitAction"
                  value="io.github.ericcanas.Idlectl1.reload"/>
    </method>

    <!-- ==================== the session agent's side ====================== -->

    <!--
      Register the calling connection as the agent for one session.

      Returns the interval, in MICROSECONDS, at which the agent must call ReportSession.
      Returned rather than agreed in advance so the two cannot be configured separately
      and drift: a daemon expecting a report every 30 s and an agent sending one every
      120 s produces a permanently indeterminate human_active and a machine that never
      sleeps, with nothing obviously wrong on either side.

      NOT polkit-checked, deliberately. Registering only ever ADDS a reason the machine
      might stay awake, so refusing to register is what a hostile caller would want.
      What an agent can claim is bounded instead: it reports idleness for its own
      session and can be told to blank its own screen, and nothing else. The uid is
      taken from the bus, never from an argument.

      The daemon remembers the caller's UNIQUE connection name and calls Blank/Unblank
      back on it. That is why the agent owns no well-known name and why an ordinary
      user cannot squat the agent interface by claiming one.
    -->
    <method name="RegisterAgent">
      <arg name="session_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="can_blank" type="b" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="interval_usec" type="t" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!--
      Report what the calling agent can see inside its own session.

      `idle_usec` is how long since the last REAL input, from ext-idle-notify-v1 on
      Wayland or the MIT-SCREEN-SAVER counter on X11. UINT64_MAX means "I am alive and
      my idle protocol is not answering". That is deliberately distinct from a very
      large idle time, and the distinction is the whole point: a large idle time PERMITS
      a sleep, and an agent whose protocol broke has observed nothing at all. The daemon
      turns UINT64_MAX into human_active = indeterminate, which vetoes every sleep
      action.

      `media_playing` is one of "true", "false", "indeterminate" or "unavailable" — the
      same four-name vocabulary the Facts property publishes, for the same reason: a
      session with no player and a player that refused to answer are not the same thing,
      and collapsing them into "false" is how a veto stops existing without anybody
      noticing.

      MPRIS is reported from here rather than read by the daemon because A ROOT DAEMON
      CANNOT CONNECT TO A USER SESSION BUS AT ALL. Measured: the connection is accepted
      at the socket and then closed during authentication, because a session bus serves
      exactly one uid and root is not special to D-Bus. No file permission changes that.
      The privileged half of this project therefore touches no session bus anywhere,
      which is a smaller attack surface as well as the only thing that works.

      `gpu_holders` is every process of the agent's own uid that holds DRM memory, as
      (pid, process name, bytes), already de-duplicated per DRM client id — a process
      holding several descriptors on one client would otherwise be counted once per
      descriptor and a 400 MiB title would read as 4 GiB.

      It is reported from here for a permission reason rather than a bus one. A process's
      fdinfo directory is mode 0555 and looks world readable, but every open is gated by
      ptrace_may_access, so reading ANOTHER user's needs CAP_SYS_PTRACE. Measured:
      with the daemon's CAP_DAC_READ_SEARCH the read is denied, and with CAP_SYS_PTRACE
      the same read succeeds. Granting a daemon that can power a machine off the ability
      to read any process's memory, in order to attribute video RAM, is not a trade worth
      making; the agent reads its own processes with no capability at all.

      The list is RAW and carries no judgement. The 512 MiB threshold, the exclusion of
      desktop shells by name, and the attribution of memory to a running game all happen
      in the daemon, from the process tree and the command lines, which are world
      readable. An unprivileged process must not be the one deciding what counts as a
      game.

      An EMPTY array means this session sees no DRM memory holder, and the daemon treats
      it exactly as it treats a machine whose DRM devices publish no memory accounting:
      the source contributed nothing. It is never doubt. An agent that is not running at
      all is already reported once through human_active, and raising a second veto for
      one cause would keep every headless machine awake forever.

      This doubles as the liveness heartbeat. It must be called at the interval
      RegisterAgent returned even when nothing has changed, because the human-presence
      signal has to DISAPPEAR on failure: while somebody is holding a controller an
      idle notifier emits nothing at all, so a crashed agent and a person playing a
      game are indistinguishable except by the missing heartbeat.

      An unregistered connection is refused rather than silently ignored, so that an
      agent whose daemon restarted learns to register again.
    -->
    <method name="ReportSession">
      <arg name="idle_usec" type="t" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="media_playing" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <!-- (pid, process name, bytes) per process of this agent's uid holding DRM memory. -->
      <arg name="gpu_holders" type="a(ust)" direction="in"/>
    </method>

    <!--
      Unregister the calling agent. Called on clean shutdown, so the daemon does not
      have to wait out a heartbeat timeout to notice.
    -->
    <method name="UnregisterAgent"/>

    <!-- ============================= signals ============================== -->

    <!--
      An action was performed. `reason` names what made it due: the block whose
      deadline expired, or, when a [when] ceiling fired, that ceiling, the file it
      came from and every floor it defeated. A ceiling that acted against a floor and
      said nothing would be worse than no ceiling at all.
    -->
    <signal name="ActionTaken">
      <arg name="action" type="s"/>
      <arg name="reason" type="s"/>
    </signal>

    <!--
      An action came due and was NOT performed, with the reasons that held it back.

      `blocks` also carries the reasons that are not blocks: each enabled fact
      currently reading indeterminate, and each configuration file that had to be
      rejected. Those hold the three sleep actions on their own, and a machine wedged
      awake by one of them has nothing in its configuration to point at.

      Emitted because "nothing happened" is otherwise invisible, and "why did my
      machine not sleep last night?" needs an answer that is not a guess.
    -->
    <signal name="ActionHeld">
      <arg name="action" type="s"/>
      <arg name="blocks" type="as"/>
    </signal>

    <!-- One or more facts changed state, as (name, state) pairs. -->
    <signal name="FactsChanged">
      <arg name="facts" type="a(ss)"/>
    </signal>

    <!-- Configuration was reloaded successfully; carries the layers applied. -->
    <signal name="PolicyReloaded">
      <arg name="layers" type="as"/>
    </signal>

  </interface>

  <!--
    THE SESSION AGENT'S INTERFACE.

    Exported by idlectl-agent on the system bus, on the object path
    /io/github/ericcanas/Idlectl1/Agent. The agent owns no well-known name: it
    registers with the daemon and the daemon calls back on its unique connection name,
    so nothing here requires a per-user name-ownership rule and an ordinary user
    cannot squat the interface by claiming a name. Only uid 0 may call these; see
    io.github.ericcanas.Idlectl1.conf.

    This is the ONE thing the agent does that is not reporting a fact, and the reason
    it has to exist: blanking a display is not a power transition and logind has no
    mechanism for it. On a Wayland session only the compositor, or whatever holds DRM
    master, can turn an output off, and a root daemon has no business being either. So
    the daemon decides and the agent, inside its own session, performs.

    The narrowness is the point. The agent can command nothing but the screen, only in
    its own session, and only when the caller is the daemon. It cannot suspend,
    hibernate or power off anything, and no configuration can give it those.

    Where no agent is running, or the session type offers no blanking mechanism,
    screen_off is UNAVAILABLE as an action: the daemon does not propose it, every
    block setting a screen_off key is inert, and Doctor() says so and names them. It
    is never silently treated as done.
  -->
  <interface name="io.github.ericcanas.Idlectl1.Agent">

    <!--
      Blank the outputs of this session. Idempotent: blanking an already-blank session
      is not an error and is not re-issued as an action by the daemon, which is what
      keeps a dark panel from starving the sleep actions.
    -->
    <method name="Blank"/>

    <!--
      Unblank. Called when an origin is re-armed by human input, and on the agent's
      own shutdown, so that stopping the agent can never leave a session dark with
      nothing left running that knows how to bring it back.
    -->
    <method name="Unblank"/>

    <!-- Whether the outputs are currently blanked by this agent. -->
    <property name="Blanked" type="b" access="read"/>

    <!--
      Whether this session offers a blanking mechanism at all. False means screen_off
      is unavailable here, and the daemon reports it as such rather than pretending.
    -->
    <property name="CanBlank" type="b" access="read"/>

  </interface>
</node>
